00:17:48.460 --> 00:17:56.420
<v Clayton Gentry>So we're really good at matching up content where we can say this episode was published on YouTube and it was also published over this RSS feed.

231
00:17:56.760 --> 00:18:04.280
<v Clayton Gentry>Nowadays, we have automated systems that do most of that matching work with human judgment still reserved for edge cases.

232
00:18:04.540 --> 00:18:11.720
<v Clayton Gentry>But earlier in our company's lifetime, this matching was often extremely manual where it would literally be me.

233
00:18:11.820 --> 00:18:20.000
<v Clayton Gentry>I would be going one by one through thousands of YouTube videos, identifying complementary audio that had been delivered over RSS and matching them up.

234
00:18:20.060 --> 00:18:24.840
<v Clayton Gentry>We even built admin UI to help a human, i.e. me, complete that process.

235
00:18:26.760 --> 00:18:33.180
<v Clayton Gentry>Really nice about that, though, was when you do things manually, you really learn the ins and outs of the data.

